#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:94
#: 0af750a8054e43f4bad60f9a44ee1137
msgid "Connecting the radios"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:96
#: 9c6264ae1f634538b04170d2d7e01ae6
msgid "The radio has interchangeable air and ground modules, meaning that you use them as a pair but it does not matter which one goes on the vehicle and which remains on the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:100
#: 2636edeaab804c13bb61257d38a89d3d
msgid "The radio has a micro-USB port, and a DF13 six-position port. The following sections explain how to connect these to autopilots and ground stations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:111
#: a2d2692edc164b5093da2dead189e62b
msgid "Connecting to Pixhawk"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:113
#: aa98ab6853224734bce18f4655464d28
msgid "Use the 6 pin DF13 connector that should have come with the radio to connect the radio to your Pixhawk's \"Telem 1\" (\"Telem 2\" or \"Serial 4/5\" can also be used but the default recommendation is \"Telem1\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:121
#: 33566524228c4106a4aa61e0e98e73f5
msgid "Connecting to a PC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:123
#: 96abdccd54b44e6ba5fd2471543a6651
msgid "Connecting the radio to your Windows PC is as simple as connecting the micro USB cable (which should have been included with the radio) to your PC. The necessary drivers should be installed automatically and the radio will appear as a new \"USB Serial Port\" in the Windows Device Manager under Ports (COM & LPT). The Mission Planner's COM Port selection drop-down should also contain the same new COM port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:133
#: 11487f6b9bc04eb587f73b9dcae1cde5
msgid "To connect the radios:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:135
#: d4c378b7eb874f1eb0cddf7286cb61f5
msgid "Select the new COM port, set the baud rate drop down (which appears between the COM port and Connect buttons) to 57600."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:137
#: 88a0224e89ba42d8946c2383aa936d24
msgid "Press the **Connect** button and if the two radios connect successfully you should be able to lean your vehicle left and right and see its attitude update on the MP's Flight Data screen's artificial horizon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:143
#: c736b1ef0542454d8f3c6d632b3f243a
msgid "Connecting to an Android tablet"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:145
#: 7f0dc4dd7ac74dbcad2170c6f53ffef7
msgid "Connecting the radio to an Android tablet using the L shaped micro USB cable (which should have been included with the radio). Then follow the directions in your :ref:`preferred Ground Station app <common-choosing-a-ground-station>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:156
#: a9281881773645fa9003501e4eeb5b05
msgid "Configuring using the Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:158
#: 875152e5186d4c07aaf0fca8e67c6255
msgid "Many users will not need to configure their radios! One case where you might do so is when you use your vehicle with others — in which case you will need to specify different radio channels (Net ID)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:162
#: 4539ba7c47ed414e876281d069b15184
msgid "Mission Planner supports configuring your radios using a simple GUI interface. For more information see :ref:`Configuring a Telemetry Radio using Mission Planner <common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:166
#: 3f59e8fb6fac4629a08448929761c6f8
msgid "Updating the Firmware"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:168
#: f251852f5a1a4841bec73bbb112dde89
msgid "You can check the firmware version (latest version is `here <https://firmware.ardupilot.org/SiK/stable/>`__ ) using the GUI tool mentioned in the previous section and update if necessary with  :ref:`these instructions<sik-firmware-update>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:171
#: db1cbd4a855746cd963604733f36807f
msgid "Expected range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:173
#: d289c9c97d6d4c70820e58014e43acf5
msgid "Typical range achieved with the radios using standard configuration and antenna is around 500m, but the range varies a lot depending on noise sources and antenna setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:178
#: 6c67fdf2381d421d8cf4acb1295c1a87
msgid "Diagnosing range issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:180
#: 3e12695aaea844b09a392e746c31c986
msgid "The first thing you should do when diagnosing range issues is the \"one meter test\". Setup the two radios one meter apart and look at the local and remote RSSI. You should get a value of over 190 for a standard SiK radio. If you don't then your antennas may be faulty or your radios may have been damaged. If you have ever run the radios without an antenna attached them the radio may have been damaged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:187
#: fda5940a96044aa8afb144e0d0d2092a
msgid "If that test passes then have a look at your local and remote RSSI and noise from a flight. See the :ref:`advanced setup page <common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information_diagnosing_range_problems>` for detailed information on diagnosing range issues using telemetry logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:193
#: 8974aca0fa6840e2b7efe1b9c50097f5
msgid "Improving the range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:195
#: 25234090a5b8466caad2d9f745d1984f
msgid "The range can be improved by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:197
#: 0ceefbe15cc84595a699d7d71650b675
msgid "Using an inexpensive 900Mhz yagi antenna on the ground like `these <https://www.ebay.com/sch/i.html?_from=R40&_trksid=p2380057.m570.l1313.TR1.TRC0.A0.H0.X900mhz+yagi.TRS2&_nkw=900mhz+yagi&_sacat=0>`__  perhaps mounted on an :ref:`Antenna Tracker <antennatracker:home>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:198
#: a8564e06b7404b48ae0706255c0641b6
msgid "Replace at least one of the two radios with a higher powered :ref:`RFD900 <common-rfd900>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:200
#: 128ab8e53d074201a7d6e9f8049495d5
msgid "Reduce the ``AirRate`` parameter which will increase range but at the expense of the rate of the data transfer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:203
#: 3852a255681340e59d25a190f3f7beaa
msgid "The graph below shows the ground and remote receiver strength vs distance from home (in meters) for a test flight of a *3DR Radio* (SiK implementation) on a SkyFun plane. The ground based radio was attached to a 3.5dB flat patch antenna. The SkyFun had a small 'wire' antenna. The graph shows the vehicle reached a distance of nearly 3km and the link was completely maintained throughout the flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-sik-telemetry-radio.rst:213
#: c7a23b7346d7489d9d4de77305b61afe
msgid "Although anecdotal, another user provided a Tlog showing a good link kept while the vehicle was 4.5km from the base station, using default radio settings for a 3DR 900 radio. The plane used a small omni antenna, and a 8dB patch antenna on the ground station."
msgstr ""

